      "## The short answer",
      "Published 2026 guides converge on roughly these bands for professionally built apps: Simple app: $7,500–$45,000 — Content, catalogue or booking-lite: a handful of screens, fed by existing content or a simple backend; Product MVP: $20,000–$135,000 — Accounts, a custom backend, payments, notifications — a real product's first version; Marketplace / platform app: $75,000+ — Two-sided (buyers and sellers), commissions, complex roles, heavy integrations.",
      "Our own mobile app development packages are published against the same ladder: a content-driven app for both stores from $4,999, a cross-platform product app with accounts, backend and payments from $9,999, and complex or native builds from $18,000. The sections below explain why the market's bands are so wide — and which band your app honestly belongs in.",
      "## The decision that moves the price most: one codebase or two",
      "A native app is built twice — once for iPhone, once for Android — in two languages, by (usually) two developers, and maintained twice forever. A cross-platform app is built once and published to both stores. For most business apps, cross-platform costs roughly 40% less to build and noticeably less to maintain, with no difference a user can feel. Native earns its premium in a narrow set of cases: heavy graphics or games, deep device features, or performance-critical media work. An honest agency will tell you which one you are before quoting — at CINEM that conversation is part of the first call, because quoting native money for a cross-platform job is the oldest trick in this market.",
      "The second decision is whether you need an app at all yet. If the product is unproven, a responsive web app answers the same questions at about half the cost, with no app-store approval and nothing to install. Many of our app projects start life as a web application and graduate to the stores once the workflow has paying users. That sequence is not a compromise; it is how you avoid spending app money on an unvalidated idea.",
      "## What actually drives the number",
      "Screens are cheap; systems are not. Ten screens fed by your existing website cost little. The same ten screens backed by accounts, payments, live data and an admin panel are a software platform that happens to have an app attached. Most app quotes are really backend quotes.",
      "Accounts and roles multiply scope. A guest-only app is one product. Add customer accounts, then a staff role, then an admin — each role is another interface, another permission model, another set of edge cases. Count your roles before you count your screens.",
      "Integrations behave like dependencies. Payments (with app-store rules on digital goods — Apple and Google take their cut and set the terms), maps, messaging, your CRM, your booking system: each is someone else's platform, with review processes and policy changes you do not control. App-store approval itself is a timeline risk that belongs in every plan: budget days, occasionally weeks, for review and resubmission.",
      "Design finish is a dial, not a default. A functional internal app and a consumer brand experience are different amounts of design work. Decide which you are buying; paying consumer-grade design prices for a staff tool is as wasteful as the reverse is damaging.",
      "## The cost after the build",
      "Apps have a running meter that websites mostly do not. Budget 15–20% of the build cost per year for maintenance: operating-system updates break things annually whether you ship features or not, store policies change, and libraries need security updates. Add the store accounts themselves (Apple's developer program is $99/year; Google Play is a one-time $25) under *your* ownership — an app published under an agency's account is an app you do not fully own. Our position is simple and written into how we work: the app, the store accounts, the code and the data are yours.",
      "Then there is the cost of being ignored: an app nobody can find is a very expensive icon. Discovery — store listing quality, reviews, and the marketing that drives installs — deserves a line in the budget conversation from the start, even if the honest conclusion is \"the app is for existing customers only,\" which is a perfectly good reason to build one.",
      "## Frequently asked questions",
      "How much does it cost to make an app in 2026?",
      "Simple apps typically cost $7,500–$45,000, product MVPs $20,000–$135,000, and marketplace apps $75,000 and up. CINEM's published packages start at $4,999 for a content-driven app, $9,999 for a cross-platform product app with backend and payments, and from $18,000 for complex or native builds.",
      "Is cross-platform or native cheaper?",
      "Cross-platform — one codebase for both stores — typically costs about 40% less to build and less to maintain. Native is worth its premium mainly for heavy graphics, deep device features or performance-critical media.",
      "How long does it take to build an app?",
      "A content-driven app usually takes 4–6 weeks; a product MVP 8–12 weeks. App-store review adds days to weeks on top and should be planned for, especially around store holidays and policy changes.",
      "How much does it cost to maintain an app?",
      "Plan on 15–20% of the build cost per year: OS updates, security patches, store policy changes and small improvements. Store accounts are $99/year (Apple) and $25 one-time (Google Play), and should always be in your name.",
      "Should I build an app or a web app first?",
      "If the idea is unproven, start with a responsive web app — it reaches every device at roughly half the cost and skips store approval. Build the native/cross-platform app once the workflow has real users, or immediately if the product needs device features or a home-screen presence for existing customers.",
      "Send us the idea in whatever shape it is in — or just tell us what your app needs to do in a paragraph. We will reply within one business day with an honest band, the cross-platform-versus-native answer for your case, and whether a web app should come first. You can also write to us directly at hello@cinem.tech, or explore our mobile app development service first.",
